    "body": "## Summary\n\n**Extends the ADR-073 `sync:` trace with `h_px` and tetris state.**\n\nThe `sync_drawing_area_size()` trace previously logged only `viewport_h` and `scroll_y`. During the \"phantom History bug\" debugging session, we needed to know:\n- What `h_px` (content height) the pipeline reported\n- What `(top, draw, bot)` tetris values would be computed\n\nWithout those fields, we couldn't tell the difference between:\n- A real layout bug (h_px wrong)\n- A tetris math bug (h_px right, tetris wrong)\n- A stale binary artifact (h_px right, tetris right, user was running old binary)\n\nThe extended trace prints the answer directly:\n\n```\nsync: viewport_h=641 h_px=31349 scroll_y=0.00 tetris=(0,641,30708)\n```\n\n## Why permanent (per ADR-073)\n\nAll three new fields (`h_px`, `top`, `draw`, `bot`) are primitives — no `std::string` allocations, no `get_attribute()` calls. The `HB_LOG` macro's internal guard is sufficient; no additional `log_enabled()` wrapper needed. **Zero cost when `HONEYBEE_LOG` is unset.**\n\nThis is exactly the ADR-073 policy: extend an existing trace with fields the trace was always missing, commit permanently, never remove.\n\n## What it proved\n\nThe extended trace resolved the phantom \"History doesn't appear\" bug in one run:\n\n- `h_px=31349` — the pipeline correctly reported Wikipedia's full content height\n- `tetris=(0,641,30708)` — the math was correct (30708 + 641 = 31349)\n- User could scroll to `scroll_y=30708` — the ScrolledWindow's cap was correct\n\n**The bug was a stale `build-release/bin/honeybee` binary** compiled before ADR-071/072/073. The debug build had all the fixes; the release build didn't. After wiping `build-release/` and reconfiguring with Ninja, the release binary renders Wikipedia correctly with all fixes.\n\n## The math is computed before early returns\n\nMoved `layout_height()` and `std::ceil` calls before the `if (viewport_h <= 0) return;` guards. `layout_height()` is a `const` getter — no side effects, safe to call unconditionally. This ensures the trace shows what *would have been used* even when the function bails out. That's essential for diagnosing \"why didn't tetris get set?\" — the state that was computed before the bail tells you what the function intended to do.\n\n## Test count\n\n694 / 2646 unchanged (trace is guarded, no behavior change).\n\n## Verification\n\nThe extended trace was used to diagnose and rule out a bug. Now committed as permanent instrumentation per ADR-073.\n\nRefs #280\n",
